.chapter 2
.h1 Probing the old program [probing]
The old program shows what it does only when it runs.  A script ran it in
DOSBox-X, an emulator of a DOS machine, and recorded keys, screens and prints.
No machine code of the old program was disassembled, and none was
copied.{fn:What was used: the manuals and the on-line help of the old program,
once the help file had been decoded as data; its sample documents; and the
names and messages that its program files hold as plain data, which showed how
its files are laid out.  What was observed: the program itself, run in the
emulator described in this chapter and driven by a script that pressed keys and
measured what appeared on the screen and in the print, one question at a time.
No machine code of the old program was disassembled.}
.h2 Set-up [probing-setup]
The set-up has four parts.
1. A scratch DOS drive: the install disks in one folder, the sample documents
in another, never the working folder of the project.
2. A private X display (Xvfb) with all output of the emulator in a file.
3. {c:xdotool} sends keys and {c:xwd} captures the window.  80 columns are 540
pixels wide, so one cell is 6.75 pixels, which resolves a single character,
rule or highlight.
4. One line of the emulator configuration, {c:parallel1=file file:PATH.prn
timeout:3000}, attaches the parallel port to a file.

The Generic driver writes plain text.  The Apple LaserWriter driver writes
PostScript, in which each string after a move to a point is text at a known
position in units of 1/300 inch.  The studies of layout and colour recovered
fonts, text and positions of whole prints from such files.
.h2 Findings [probing-learned]
{Tab:settled} lists questions that the probes settled and that changed the
program.
.tabcap settled Questions settled by the probes
.table 30 34
Question | Answer
Are stored tab widths a layout fault? | No.  The old program lays a tab out
 | again from the tab stops.
How wide is a table column? | Its own width plus the empty cells at its
 | left and right.
Is there a default level indent? | No.  Without a setting sections are not
 | indented.
Is there a border on columns? | Yes.  Box lines fill the empty cells
 | beside the columns.
.end
The first guess for the level indent, 3 cells, was wrong; a probe of a document
without a setting showed no indent.  The notes keep the wrong guess with its
correction.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.h2 Defects of the set-up [probing-trouble]
Four defects cost time and changed later probes.
- After every dialog the window needs a mouse click to regain the focus.
- {k:Ctrl-F2} (Math) and {k:Ctrl-F6} (Case) never reached the old program.  The
  study of Math quotes the help text and says "not probed".
- Files made after the emulator started stay invisible to the menu of the
  dictionaries until it restarts.
- In the Border Layout panel {k:Backspace} must clear a number field before
  typing replaces its text.
.endif
.h2 Limits of the probes [probing-limits]
A probe shows the cases that were tried.  The notes tag each statement by its
evidence: seen on the screen or in a file, read in the help of the old
program, inferred, or guessed.  Features that were never tried stay open
questions in the notes.
.note The manuals of release 1 of the old program were available as text.  The
  manual of release 2 was not, only its help file.  Questions that only that
  manual could answer stay open.
